1. Class & Object
Definition
Class:
A blueprint or template that defines properties (variables) and behaviors (methods).
Logical entity.

Object:
A real-time instance of a class.
Physical entity.

class Car {
    String brand;
    void drive() {
        System.out.println("Driving");
    }
}

Car car = new Car();

Interview Questions

Q1. What is the difference between class and object?
👉 Class is a blueprint; object is an instance of that blueprint.

Q2. Can a class exist without object?
👉 Yes.

Q3. Can an object exist without class?
👉 No.

Tricky Questions
How many objects are created when a class is loaded? → Zero
Where does object live in memory? → Heap
Where does class metadata live? → Metaspace (Java 8+)

2. Encapsulation
Definition
Wrapping data and methods together into a single unit.
Achieved using private variables + public methods.

class Person {
    private int age;

    public void setAge(int age) {
        if (age > 0)
            this.age = age;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}

Interview Questions

Q1. What is encapsulation?
👉 Data hiding + controlled access.

Q2. Why encapsulation is important?
👉 Security, validation, maintainability.

Tricky Questions

Is encapsulation only data hiding? → No
Can we achieve encapsulation without getters/setters? → Yes (immutable class)

3. Inheritance
Definition
A mechanism where one class acquires properties and behavior of another class.
Achieved using extends.

Types of Inheritance
Single Inheritance
class A {}
class B extends A {}

Multilevel Inheritance
class A {}
class B extends A {}
class C extends B {}

Hierarchical Inheritance
class A {}
class B extends A {}
class C extends A {}

❌ Java does not support multiple inheritance using classes.

Interview Questions

Q1. Why Java doesn’t support multiple inheritance?
👉 Ambiguity problem (diamond problem).

Q2. Are constructors inherited?
👉 No.

Tricky Questions

Are private members inherited? → Yes, but not accessible
Can final class be inherited? → No
Can inheritance increase coupling? → Yes

4. Polymorphism
Definition
One method, many forms.
Allows behavior to change at runtime.

Method Overloading (Compile-Time Polymorphism)
Same method name
Different parameter list

int add(int a, int b) {}
double add(double a, double b) {}

Interview Questions

Q. Can we overload static methods?
👉 Yes.

Tricky Questions
Can overloading happen by return type only? → No
Does method overloading depend on access modifiers? → No

Method Overriding (Runtime Polymorphism)
Same method signature
Child class implementation

class Parent {
    void show() {}
}

class Child extends Parent {
    void show() {}
}

Interview Questions
Q. What is runtime polymorphism?
👉 Method call resolved at runtime using object reference.

Tricky Questions:
Can static methods be overridden? → No
Can private methods be overridden? → No
Can we reduce visibility while overriding? → No

5. Abstraction
Definition
Hiding implementation details and showing only essential features.

Abstract Class
abstract class Vehicle {
    abstract void start();
}

✔ Can have variables
✔ Can have constructors
✔ Partial abstraction

Interface
interface Payment {
    void pay();
}

✔ Supports multiple inheritance
✔ Java 8 → default & static methods
✔ Complete abstraction (Java 7)

Abstract Class vs Interface (Very Important)
| Abstract Class       | Interface            |
| -------------------- | -------------------- |
| Partial abstraction  | Full abstraction     |
| Can have constructor | Cannot               |
| Single inheritance   | Multiple inheritance |

Tricky Questions
Can abstract class have constructor? → Yes
Can interface have variables? → Yes (public static final)

6. Association, Aggregation, Composition
Association
General relationship between classes.

class Student {
    Teacher teacher;
}

Aggregation (Weak HAS-A):
Objects can exist independently.

Composition (Strong HAS-A):
Child object cannot exist without parent.

class Engine {}
class Car {
    Engine engine = new Engine();
}

Tricky Questions
Difference between aggregation & composition?
👉 Object lifecycle dependency

7. this Keyword
Definition:
Refers to current object.
this.age = age;

Uses:
Resolving variable shadowing
Calling constructor (this())

Tricky Questions:
Can this be used in static context? → No
Can this() and super() be used together? → No

8. super Keyword
Definition:
Refers to parent class object.

Uses:
Access parent methods
Call parent constructor
super();

Tricky Questions
Is super() implicit? → Yes
Can we access private members using super? → No

9. instanceof
Definition:

Used to check object type at runtime.
if(obj instanceof Employee)

Interview Questions
Q. When is instanceof used?
👉 Safe type casting.

Tricky Questions
Can instanceof be used with interfaces? → Yes
Does instanceof work with null? → Always false
